WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.160
Maybe that mild-mannered dude in the corner
was really the big bad guy.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.440
Perhaps the hero was dead the whole time.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.580
Or maybe everything we've seen is somebody's
hallucination.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.360
Whatever the reveal, these memorable movie
moments caught us off guard and left us talking

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.500
for years to come.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.340
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.480
Usually, movies are told in chronological
order, but much like its protagonist, Dr.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.560
Louise Banks, Arrival isn't confined to a
single time.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.720
In the movie's opening, we watch Louise lose
her beloved daughter to disease.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.500
Naturally, we assume this is happening early
in her life, well before the renowned linguist

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.120
is asked by the military to communicate with
the heptapods mysterious aliens who've landed

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:39.620
on Earth.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.370
Her job is to translate their complex language
so humans can talk with these otherworldly

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.000
visitors.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:49.860
As she delves deeper into their circular alphabet,
though, the heptapod language changes her

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.800
perspective of time, allowing her to experience
the past, present, and future at once.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.540
Before long, we discover that Louise actually
hasn't had her child yet.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.911
Those flashbacks are actually flash-forwards,
visions of what's to come, courtesy of the

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.060
alien language.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.020
Louise now exists outside of time, or more
accurately, throughout every moment simultaneously.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.900
As a result, her perspective on concepts like
love are radically altered by the heptapods'

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:17.400
gift.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.240
Sure, she now knows that heartbreak is coming,
but she heads into the future anyway, because

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.440
she knows there's a lot of joy waiting there
too.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.060
It might just be the most emotional twist
in all of science fiction, and one that will

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.780
leave you both thinking and wiping away a
few tears.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.840
A Beautiful Mind finds an Oscar-nominated
Russell Crowe playing John Nash, a real-life

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.280
Nobel Prize-winning mathematician who lived
a rather unusual life.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.120
Early in the film, Nash is drafted by Department
of Defense Agent William Parcher to analyze

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.960
magazines and newspapers for hidden Russian
codes.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.560
"You are, quite simply, the best natural codebreaker
I've ever seen."

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.591
It's an intense assignment, but when he's
not engaged in high-stakes espionage, John

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.300
seems to lead a normal life with a supportive
wife and an incredibly close BFF, Charles.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.580
Whenever Charles shows up to visit, he brings
along his adorable niece, Marcee, who always

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.160
brings a smile to John's face.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.540
Unfortunately, of the four most important
people in John's life, only one of them is

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.860
actually a living, breathing human being.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.180
In reality, John is suffering from paranoid
schizophrenia, and the incredibly creepy Parcher

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.840
is just a hallucination.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:29.220
There are no secret messages, no Soviet spies
lurking in the shadows, and no radium implanted

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:29.820
in his arm.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.640
The real blow, however, is that Charles and
Marcee are also products of his imagination.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.099
Unless you know the true story, the twist
comes as a devastating surprise and sends

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.020
this Academy Award-winning film in a disturbing
direction.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.000
Colossal is one of the strangest movies ever
made.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.099
This fantasy flick finds Anne Hathaway as
the alcoholic Gloria, recently single and

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.310
moving back to her hometown.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.060
But as the self-destructive woman soon discovers,
her problems are a lot bigger than just the

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:57.560
bottle.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:03.500
Gloria is psychically linked to a giant, Godzilla-like
monster, and whenever she stumbles across

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.060
a particular playground, the kaiju mysteriously
appears in the city of Seoul, South Korea.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.320
"What's it doing?"

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:11.920
"It's dancing!"

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.400
"It's dancing like…?"

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.820
Fortunately, Gloria has a good friend to help
with all the booze and oversized beasts.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.960
Jason Sudeikis plays her childhood friend,
a nice guy named Oscar.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.460
The moment Gloria comes back to town, Oscar
does everything he can to help her feel at

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:28.960
home.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.300
He gives her furniture for her empty house,
builds her up with all sorts of compliments,

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:37.299
and even gives her a job working at his bar,
which is probably not the best place for an

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.419
alcoholic to work.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.820
But then Gloria realizes that there's a lot
of darkness behind Oscar's winning smile.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.480
For one, he's also linked to another giant
creature that appears in the South Korean

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:47.980
capital.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.820
And while he pretends to be a good dude, we
quickly learn he's a violent, manipulative

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.620
control freak, more monstrous than any kaiju.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:00.459
And when Gloria tries to get away from this
creep, he threatens to murder countless Koreans

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:01.639
with his robot avatar.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:08.220
"Either you drink that beer or I'll go take
a walk through the park later."

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.460
This turn from best friend to abuser is pretty
shocking, largely thanks to Sudeikis' affable

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.060
persona.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.240
That said, there were probably a lot of women
who saw this coming, well aware that the real

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.100
world is full of Oscars.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.840
One of the most unsettling sci-fi movies ever
made, Ex Machina finds a mild-mannered computer

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.740
programmer named Caleb winning the contest
of a lifetime.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:34.340
He's been picked to spend a week conducting
a bizarre Turing test with his billionaire

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.780
boss, Nathan, played here by a super creepy,
show-stealing Oscar Isaac.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.419
A brilliant inventor, Nathan has created an
artificial intelligence named Ava, and he

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.080
wants to see if his creation can convince
Caleb she's a sentient being.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.620
Caleb quickly falls under Ava's spell, and
soon, the programmer plans to free his crush

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:52.880
from Nathan's grasp.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.300
"Do you have people who test you and might
switch you off?"

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:57.800
“no.”

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:58.660
"Then why do I?"

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:03.220
After all, the tech tycoon has her locked
up and plans on essentially killing her.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.240
Plus, Nathan's interest in Ava isn't just
scientific this guy has a thing for building

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.440
female robots that he can imprison and control.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:17.500
Using his own tech savvy, Caleb sets Ava free,
and seconds later, the liberated android slices

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.880
through Nathan like he's a pat of hot butter.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.080
If this were your typical sci-fi flick, Ava
and Caleb would probably run off together,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:30.189
but in Ex Machina, things take a hard left
turn when Ava locks up Caleb and leaves the

00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:32.620
screaming programmer to die of starvation.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.419
It's a gut punch of an ending, especially
since Caleb seems so sympathetic.

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:41.349
Sadly, there's a good chance that he would've
jeopardized her shot at freedom in the outside

00:05:41.349 --> 00:05:41.840
world.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.780
In the end, he had to be tested and switched
off after all.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.080
It's no surprise that the Armitage family
are the bad guys in Get Out.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.740
When Missy Armitage and her husband Dean first
meet our everyman hero, Chris Washington,

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.430
they're way too friendly and far too polite,
which is never a good sign in a horror movie.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.480
"By the way, I would've voted for Obama for
a third term best President in my lifetime

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.140
hands down."

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.200
"I agree."

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.760
Then there's their super creepy son, Jeremy,
who's oozing with menace whenever he comes

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.180
slinking onto the screen.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.919
But with all these racists surrounding poor
Chris, his girlfriend Rose seems totally on

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:19.440
his side.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.220
She'll definitely help him escape her family's
clutches, right?

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.720
Unfortunately, Rose is probably the most evil
member of the entire family.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.460
After finding a box of incriminating photos,
Chris starts to realize he's the victim of

00:06:32.469 --> 00:06:37.150
some really cruel catfishing, but he still
desperately hopes that Rose is on his side

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:39.140
when the Armitage clan blocks his path.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.900
He begs Rose to give him the car keys so he
can escape, and for a moment, it seems like

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.860
she might pick her boyfriend over her own
flesh and blood.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:47.920
She doesn't.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:49.780
"Where are those keys, Rose?"

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.580
"You know I can't give you the keys right,
babe?"

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.340
The casting here is what makes the twist work
so well.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.300
According to both Allison Williams and director
Jordan Peele, Williams was cast because audiences

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.460
would associate her with the far less evil
roles that she'd played before.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:12.480
Sure enough, she does indeed seem pretty harmless...
right up until she doesn't.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.880
Messing around with creepy European cults
is never a good idea.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:21.620
That's a lesson our murderous anti-hero learns
the hard way in Ben Wheatley's Kill List.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.200
This disturbing horror film is centered around
Jay, a family man whose marriage has fallen

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:27.210
on hard times.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.960
And, oh yeah, he makes his living as a hired
killer.

00:07:30.509 --> 00:07:34.300
You've got to put food on the table somehow,
and Jay stands to make a tidy paycheck by

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:38.480
killing three people for a mysterious client
who wants his contract signed in blood.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.580
The weird thing is, the targets don't behave
like typical victims.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:47.160
Instead of begging for their lives, they actually
thank Jay when he shows up for some hammer

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:47.660
time.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:52.840
Things go from eerie to straight-up hellish
when Jay tries to kill his third victim.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:57.920
Instead of finding his target, he stumbles
into a cult right out of The Wicker Man.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:02.319
This whole assassination scheme was part of
an elaborate occult ritual, and it comes to

00:08:02.319 --> 00:08:07.039
a bloody end when the pagans capture Jay,
put a mask over his face, and force him to

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.340
fight a cloaked hunchback.

00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:13.640
The assassin makes short work of his opponent,
but after turning this guy into a shish kebab,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:19.319
Jay discovers a horrific truth the hunchback
is really his wife and son, kidnapped by the

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.820
cult and tied together, with his son on his
wife's back.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:28.110
Jay's just murdered his own family, much to
the delight of the deranged cult, capping

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.139
off a ghastly film with a truly gruesome ending.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.000
Bong Joon-ho's Mother has a pretty standard
plot for a murder mystery.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.000
A mentally challenged young man is accused
of murdering a teenage girl.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.150
The police don't have any solid evidence against
him, but they need to make an arrest, so they

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:47.340
toss the disabled man in jail and trick him
into confessing.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.640
Needless to say, his devoted mother knows
her son is innocent, and she goes from a mild-mannered

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:58.160
widow to South Korea's Sherlock Holmes, chasing
down leads, interrogating suspects, and putting

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.200
together a case to free her son from jail.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:05.040
She even discovers a possible scheme involving
blackmail and a mysterious old man.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:09.400
Now, in your typical murder mystery, the mom
would discover the killer's true identity

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:10.860
and clear her kid's name.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:16.770
However, Mother subverts all those expectations
when the titular mom makes a simple but shocking

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:18.540
discovery: her son really did it.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:23.850
Yeah, he's a sweet kid, and yes, he's mentally
disabled, but that didn't stop him from caving

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:24.840
in a girl's head.

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:30.000
It's a smart little twist that gets even twistier
when the mom murders an eyewitness to protect

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:30.640
her son.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:35.920
Mother spins every thriller trope on its head,
but it also goes to show that nobody loves

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:36.840
you like your mother.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:41.440
Every knife-wielding villain in a horror movie
has a special talent for taking out their

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:47.690
victims when they least expect it, but Ghostface,
the sinister slasher from 1996's Scream, seems

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.140
like he could pop up anywhere at any time.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.260
When the masked killer first arrives in the
opening scene after terrorizing poor Drew

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:58.320
Barrymore over the phone, he seems to be lurking
around every single corner.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.790
There's no way anyone can move this quickly,
but as victim after victim learns, there's

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.440
no getting away from Ghostface.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:05.760
This dude is everywhere.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:07.500
"Please don't kill me, Mr. Ghostface!

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:09.100
I wanna be in the sequel!"

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:15.140
In the big climax of Wes Craven's horror classic,
we discover the secret behind Ghostface's

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:16.460
teleporting powers.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.660
Final girl Sidney Prescott is running for
her life.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:23.920
The masked murderer has shown up at a high
school party, and he's slicing people left

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:24.420
and right.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:28.620
Sidney has no clue who's under the mask, and
we have plenty of suspects.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:34.560
Ghostface could be boyfriend Billy, horror
movie fan Randy, goofball Stu, or even Sidney's

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:35.320
own dad.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.140
There are so many suspects, and it's impossible
to know who to trust.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:45.960
And that's when Sidney makes a horrific discovery:
her boyfriend Billy is Ghostface… and so

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.040
is his best bud Stu.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:51.800
These two psychos are working together for
a maximum kill count and providing each other

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:52.580
with an alibi.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:54.560
"Movies don't create psychos!

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.120
Movies make psychos more creative!"

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:03.140
With two guys running around wearing Ghostface
masks, it's no wonder this bad guy was making

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:04.140
such good time.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.780
M. Night Shyamalan's extremely weird filmography
has made him a punchline for many moviegoers,

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:15.500
but he'll always have one indisputable claim
to fame: he made The Sixth Sense, an all-time

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:19.540
great horror movie with possibly the most
famous twist in cinematic history.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.520
The film finds Bruce Willis playing Dr. Malcolm
Crowe, an award-winning child psychologist.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.180
In the opening scene, he's shot by a disturbed
patient he couldn't save, but a couple of

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.460
months later, Crowe is walking around and
seems perfectly okay.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:36.940
Well, his marriage is falling apart, and his
wife won't acknowledge his existence, so that

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:37.440
isn't great.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:42.680
But at least he gets a chance at redemption
by helping Cole Sear, a troubled boy who can…

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:43.520
well, you know.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.100
"I see dead people."

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:50.420
However, Cole isn't the only one who can see
spirits.

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:53.920
The audience has been looking at a dead guy
since the start of the movie.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:59.460
After trying so hard to talk to his wife,
Crowe finally realizes why she won't respond:

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:00.740
he's a ghost.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:05.540
That gunshot in the beginning of the film
was actually fatal, and he's been a dead man

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:09.000
walking ever since, despite never realizing
it himself.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:13.720
It was an ending that sent shockwaves through
Hollywood, and Shyamalan has been trying to

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:15.350
recapture that glory ever since.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:21.020
But while a film or two has come close, The
Sixth Sense still stands supreme as the perfect

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:22.080
Shyamalan twist.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:25.220
Everybody knows the twist from The Empire
Strikes Back.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:30.680
Even moviegoers who have never seen a single
Star Wars movie know Darth Vader is actually

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:32.240
Luke Skywalker's dad.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:38.060
The twist has permeated the pop culture consciousness,
and given us one of the most memorable, and

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.100
misquoted, lines of all-time.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.700
"la la la Luke…

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:45.120
Luke…

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.680
I am your father!

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:53.120
Back in 1980, however, the Luke-Vader revelation
absolutely floored Star Wars fans.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:56.900
Most of the cast and crew didn't even know
the truth about Luke's family tree.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.060
According to Mark Hamill, the original script
read differently.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.410
"The way we filmed it…

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:04.100
Vader said You don’t know the truth.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:06.680
Obi-Wan killed your father."

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:12.440
Famously, only Hamill, James Earl Jones, George
Lucas, and director Irvin Kershner were aware

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.320
of the twist.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:18.980
So when Vader finally announced his parentage,
everybody was gasping, from audiences to the

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:19.980
actors themselves.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:23.820
And this shocking moment did a whole lot more
than elicit gasps.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:28.710
The Dad Vader reveal sent the series in a
completely new direction, and laid the groundwork

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.330
for countless additional Star Wars films.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:34.420
Has any other movie twist had such an enormous
impact?

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:39.640
The Last Jedi is one of the twistiest chapters
in the entire Star Wars franchise.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:42.670
We learn that Rey's parents are nobodies.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:44.320
Big bad Snoke gets cut in half.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.220
Luke was a Force-induced illusion the whole
time!

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:52.920
It's twist after crazy twist, but the biggest
shock comes early in the film, when Luke Skywalker

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:54.560
reveals his Jedi days are done.

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:59.910
To set the scene, we have to go back a long,
long time ago to 2015, when The Force Awakens

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:02.620
was teasing audiences with the promise of
Luke Skywalker.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.860
It had been over 30 years since fans had seen
the beloved Jedi Knight, and the entire premise

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.920
of Episode 7 involved a group of heroes searching
for the legendary warrior.

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:16.920
Finally, at the end of the film, Rey finds
the bearded Skywalker standing on a majestic

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:17.420
cliff.

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:23.040
As that John Williams music starts to swell,
Rey hands the Jedi his long lost lightsaber,

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:27.280
and that's where the film ends with Rey reaching
out to the hooded, silent hero.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:31.380
Audiences had to wait two whole years to see
what would happen, but obviously, Luke was

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.880
going to take the lightsaber and lead the
charge against the First Order… right?

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:37.100
Not exactly.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:41.390
In the years since we last saw him, Luke has
turned his back on the Force and wants the

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:42.520
Jedi to die out.

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:46.500
This little twist flew in the face of fans'
expectations, but really, they should've listened

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:51.500
when Yoda told us all those years ago that
the Jedi were never meant to be great warriors.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:57.340
If Terry Gilliam, Karl Marx, Spike Lee, and
Rod Serling all teamed up to make a movie,

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:59.340
it might look like Sorry to Bother You.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:04.460
Written and directed by Boots Riley, this
movie is absolutely insane in all the right

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.820
ways, and no description can do it justice.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.440
It is without a doubt the most anti-capitalist
film to ever come out of Hollywood, and it

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.140
features perhaps the most deranged twist of
the 21st century so far.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:20.520
The plot follows a promising young telemarketer
named Cassius "Cash" Green, who finds success

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:24.720
in sales by smooth-talking his customers using
what the film calls his "white voice."

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:25.800
"Like this, youngblood"

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:29.200
“Hey Mr. Kramer, this is Langston from Regal
View.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:31.720
"I didn't catch you at the wrong time, did
I?"

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:36.720
Soon, Cash is promoted to the big-time, which
involves selling slaves to giant companies,

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:41.210
and eventually, he crosses paths with a psycho
billionaire played by Armie Hammer with big

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:43.020
plans for the future of business.

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:48.220
Up until this point, the movie has been incredibly
weird, but when Cash discovers the billionaire's

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:51.120
plans, the film absolutely loses its mind.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:56.490
As it turns out, the billionaire is turning
people into half-human, half-horse monsters

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.660
so he can create a powerful but malleable
workforce.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:00.840
That's right.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:02.820
Armie Hammer is turning people into horses.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:07.800
And if that wasn't wild enough, the movie
ends with Cash suddenly turning into an "equisapien"

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:08.300
himself.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:13.520
It's totally bonkers, and no matter how good
you are at guessing twists, you'll never see

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:14.680
this nightmare coming.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:20.680
From the Mandarin reveal in Iron Man 3 to
the Thanos snap in Infinity War, the MCU has

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.480
its fair share of shocking twists.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:27.060
One of its most jaw-dropping moments, however,
comes in Spider-Man: Homecoming, when Peter

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:31.180
Parker picks up his date for the Homecoming
dance and finds something way more intimidating

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:32.500
than the average suburban dad.

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:36.800
Throughout this superhero flick, the wall-crawler
is trying to find a balance between homework

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:40.520
and crime fighting, hanging out with friends
and trading punches with bad guys.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:44.660
You can't really go to parties or participate
in the academic decathlon when you've got

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:48.780
an entire neighborhood to protect, and it's
hard to get a date when you're constantly

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:53.530
trying to fight the Vulture, a winged thief
who sells weapons made with alien technology

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:54.740
to local thugs.

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:59.240
Eventually, Peter puts his crime-fighting
on pause and works up the nerve to ask his

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:01.120
crush, Liz Allan, to the dance.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:05.940
He gets his suit, learns to dance, buys Liz
a nice corsage, and shows up at her house,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.080
ready for a fun evening.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:10.760
That's when the door opens to reveal the Vulture
himself.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:15.040
As it turns out, this murderous villain just
so happens to be Liz's father.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.780
Suddenly, Peter isn't worried about making
a good impression.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.740
He's just worried about making it through
the night alive, which becomes even more difficult

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:26.400
when the Vulture realizes why Peter's acting
even more nervous than the usual high school

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:28.060
kid meeting his girlfriend's father.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.100
"Very lucky he was there that day."

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:35.460
"Good ol' Spider-Man."

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:42.040
Like Uncle Ben always used to say, "with great
power comes a really complicated love life."

